{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And if there be in a man a sin, etc.</b> This passage is juxtaposed to the one before it, because it was setting forth the order of a person's rectification. First it admonished him regarding himself, as I explained in the allusion in parashat Ki Teitzei; afterward the father was admonished regarding the son, as we alluded in the verse ״כִּי יִהְיֶה בֵּן סוֹרֵר״ etc.; and here it admonishes the sages of the generation to keep a watchful eye on evildoers. That is its saying <b>if there be in a man a sin worthy of the death penalty</b> — and he remained standing in his rebellion and did not return in repentance until he died in his sin, which is its saying <b>and he is put to death</b>. And HaShem held his failing against the sage, as His saying <b>and you shall hang him on a tree</b> — this sage is a tree bearing fruit who must bear his punishment, for HaShem commanded him to reprove and to go back and reprove until striking (Arakhin 16b), and as we wrote in parashat Kedoshim. And when he closed his eye from this man until he died, he is held liable for him." "And His saying <b>you shall not leave [him] overnight, etc.</b>: It comes to command the multitudes of the people not to harbor suspicions about the sage, along the lines of our Sages' saying (Tanna deVei Eliyahu, ch. 3): If you saw a sage who transgressed a sin by day, do not harbor suspicions about him by night, for perhaps he did repentance — and they established it as certain that he did repentance. And that is its saying <b>you shall not leave his corpse</b>, for the sin is also called a נְבֵלָה (corpse): do not harbor suspicions about him at night, which is the time of overnight lodging on the tree — namely the sage — <b>for you shall surely bury him</b>: that sin, from upon the tree, on that very day. And the reason is <b>for a curse of Elohim is he hung</b> — that if you do not so, you profane the Name of Heaven, and it is as though you touch His honor, may He be blessed." "<b>And the verse concludes</b>, saying <b>and you shall not defile your land</b>: This is explained along the lines of our Sages' saying, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 119b): Jerusalem was not destroyed except because they used to disgrace Torah scholars in it, as it is said (II Chronicles 36:16) ״וַיִּהְיוּ מַלְעִיבִים״ etc. Therefore HaShem commanded that the impurity of the land — through the entry of gentiles into the heritage of HaShem — should not circulate in the disgrace of a sage, and defile it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:37.886709572Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}